+
+- **Symptom:** with the guest in files on an iPad, `[RED] [mem] guest file: 5.0 GB free on its disk, under 5.0: stopping the game` after a few minutes of play. The files held 6.6 GB on disk (`allocated on disk` in the `[mem] guest file` line) while the heap was 6.2 GB and the alias region had 2.5 GB mapped, though mapped up to +3.8 GB. The disk's free space went from 12.0 to 5.0 GB, almost all of it the files.
+- **Cause:** two. (1) What the game gave back only went `PROT_NONE` (`guest_release`): its pages kept their disk blocks for the rest of the run, and a range mapped again elsewhere took new ones. (2) The stop rule read `f_bavail` alone, which leaves out what the system can purge, against a 5 GB margin far larger than what the files could still take. iOS writes dirty file pages out within seconds of the game touching them (the iPad's files went from 0 to 4 GB on disk in 10 s), so on iOS the files' size follows what the game touches. The Mac writes nothing back unless it needs the room (0 GB allocated after 30 minutes).
+- **Fix:** general (`hle_svc.c`). `guest_file_punch` gives a range's blocks back with `F_PUNCHHOLE`. Over a mapping that also drops the pages, written out or still dirty, and they read 0 again. A wired page, under a Metal no-copy buffer the GPU still holds, keeps its bytes, so ranges are still zeroed when opened again. The heap's given-back ranges are punched at once. The alias region's are only marked: the game unmaps and maps about 0.5 GB of the same ranges a second (Ocarina of Time), and punching those at once makes every page fault back in from a hole. A sweep every 10 s (`guest_file_sweep`, under the map lock) punches the pages still given back after a whole interval. `DM_GUEST_FILE_PUNCH=0` keeps the blocks. The stop rule counts the room: free space less the files' dirty pages, counted every 10 s with `mincore` over what the game has opened (about 20 ms a gigabyte of address space; `SEEK_DATA` cannot tell which dirty pages already have blocks, as APFS reports a file with dirty mapped pages as all data). The game stops under `DM_GUEST_FILE_MIN_FREE`, now 2 GB by default. When `f_bavail` alone is short, the free space with what the system can purge counts (`kCFURLVolumeAvailableCapacityForImportantUsageKey`, at most every 10 s, as the query goes to a system service), as long as `f_bavail` keeps half the minimum, since the system purges only as the disk fills. The 10-s line adds the dirty bytes, what was given back as holes and the purgeable figure. `DM_GUEST_FILE_SCAN=1` adds the dirty pages by region and how many of them read all 0. `t_svc_memory` (`file_sweep`, under `make -C dm/host/tests memory-file`) checks that the sweep punches only pages given back over a whole interval and that they read 0 when mapped again.
+- **Why stop at all:** a full disk is worse. Measured on the Mac with a 300 MB APFS image and 600 MB of a shared file mapping written, `msync` returns 0 with 291 MB written, the other 309 MB stay dirty in memory for good (`mincore`), and every other write to the disk fails with `ENOSPC`. On a phone that is memory taken from everything else until something is killed, and saves, the shader cache and the system's own writes fail.
+- **Result on Ocarina of Time,** 5 minutes on an M4 Pro Mac, where the dirty bytes are what the files would hold on an iPad: 7.80 GB without punching, 5.98 GB with the sweep (1.84 GB given back as holes; the alias region's dirty pages 4.56 → 2.87 GB). Punching at once gave 6.32 GB, with 87 GB punched in 3 minutes. Frame time and CPU were the same with the sweep (p50 about 21 ms, GPU-bound). The game's own zeroing leaves about 770 MB of dirty heap pages that read all 0. Punching those would need the write tracker's help (a write between the check and the punch would be lost), so it is not done.
+- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md) on an iPad Pro (M5, iOS 27, 12.5 GB of guest files). The stop rule and the sweep have not run on an iPad yet.
+
 ### The guest copy of uploaded textures given back
 
 - **Symptom:** a game's footprint stays gigabytes above what it samples: the host's private Metal textures hold the content, and the guest pages the uploads read stay dirty or compressed beside them. Ocarina of Time on a 24 GB Mac: about 3.1 GB of guest memory is texture storage, 2.8 GB of it dirty or compressed (1.8 GB in CPU-visible pools whose textures are never uploaded again, 1.3 to 2.2 GB in `CPU_NO_ACCESS` pools of render and depth targets), and the run is stopped for memory pressure at gameplay entry.
